WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 153: Benjamin Harrison and the Billion-Dollar Congress: The Centennial President (1889–1893)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.640
[Rebecca] Today we step into the Gilded Age presidency of Benjamin Harrison.

4
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:28.240
[Rebecca] Often remembered as a cold, aloof figure nicknamed the Human Iceberg, Harrison was in truth an extraordinarily gifted legal scholar, a courageous Union combat general, and an activist president whose legislative partnership with Congress reshaped American corporate law and the Western Frontier.

5
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.200
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

6
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:49.680
[George] Born on a sprawling farm along the Ohio River in North Bend, Ohio, in 1833, Harrison carried the weight of history in his name.

7
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:57.280
[George] His great-grandfather, Benjamin Harrison V, was a signer of the Declaration of Independence and Governor of Virginia.

8
00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:04.720
[George] His grandfather, William Henry Harrison, was the frontier hero of Tippicano and the ninth president of the United States.

9
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:16.560
[George] His father, John Scott Harrison, was a respected two-term congressman, making John Scott the only man in American history who was both the son of one president and the father of another.

10
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:27.600
[George] Yet, despite his illustrious lineage, young Benjamin grew up in modest rural circumstances, splitting wood, milking cows, and studying under the glow of tallow candles.

11
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:41.120
[George] Educated at Miami University in Oxford, Ohio, Harrison graduated with high honors, studied law in Cincinnati, and moved to Indianapolis in 1854 with his young bride Caroline Scott.

12
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.920
[George] Harrison established himself as the finest appellate trial lawyer in Indiana.

13
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:58.640
[George] Short, compact, with piercing blue eyes and a formidable memory, Harrison was a master of courtroom argument whose forensic cross-examinations were so surgical that opposing attorneys dreaded facing him.

14
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:18.920
[George] He was deeply religious, unbendingly honest, and possessed an intense, quiet personal dignity, backslash and backslash and Benjamin Harrison inherited the stoic, unyielding Calvinist piety of his Scottish-Irish ancestors, viewing public office as a divine obligation to be performed with rigorous intellectual discipline.

15
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:27.080
[George] He held morning family prayers in the executive mansion every day without fail, expecting all household members to participate.

16
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.960
[George] When the Civil War erupted in 1861, Indiana Governor Oliver P.

17
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.760
[George] Morton summoned Harrison to the executive office and asked him to raise a regiment.

18
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:43.960
[George] Harrison had a thriving legal practice and young children, but he felt an absolute patriotic obligation to serve.

19
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:54.280
[George] He personally recruited and organized the 70th Indiana Volunteer Infantry, drilling his men with relentless discipline in camp until they operated like clockwork.

20
00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:05.160
[George] In 1864, during William Tecumseh Sherman's grueling Atlanta campaign, Colonel Benjamin Harrison proved himself a battlefield warrior of extraordinary courage.

21
00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:16.280
[George] At the Battle of Peachtree Creek on July 20, 1864, Confederate forces launched a furious surprise counterattack that smashed into an exposed gap in the Union line.

22
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:26.600
[George] Without waiting for orders, Harrison galloped to the front of his regiment, drew his saber, and led a savage bayonet charge directly into the face of Confederate musketry.

23
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:33.880
[George] Fighting hand to hand with clubbed rifles and swords, Harrison's hoosiers held the line and captured the Confederate colors.

24
00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:40.280
[George] General Joseph Hooker rode up to Harrison after the battle and exclaimed, General Harrison, by God.

25
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.360
[George] You have saved this army.

26
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:50.960
[George] Lincoln promoted him to brevet Brigadier general at age 31, backslash and backslash and throughout his service in the Atlanta campaign.

27
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:58.240
[George] General Harrison shared every hardship with his infantrymen, sleeping in the mud and personally dressing wounds on the front line.

28
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.480
[George] His men revered him not for warm camaraderie, but for his absolute fearlessness under fire.

29
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:17.120
[George] Elected to the United States Senate in 1881 and chosen as the Republican nominee for president in 1888, Harrison defeated Grover Cleveland in an intense campaign fought over the protective tariff.

30
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:26.080
[George] Taking the oath on March 4, 1889, during a pouring rainstorm, Harrison stood beneath George Washington's centennial shadow.

31
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.080
[George] Yet Harrison quickly acquired an unfortunate public reputation in Washington for personal coldness.

32
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:42.560
[George] While he was warm, affectionate, and deeply playful with his children and grandchildren in private, in public receptions he was stiff, aloof, and painfully formal.

33
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:53.280
[George] He despised political small talk, refused to shake hands when he could avoid it, and dismissed sycophantic office seekers with a frosty, silent stare that made them shiver.

34
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.040
[George] Political journalists and disgruntled party bosses quickly nicknamed him the human iceberg.

35
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.440
[George] One prominent Republican remarked.

36
00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.920
[George] Harrison can speak to an audience of 10,000 people and enchant every one of them with his brilliant oratory.

37
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:14.080
[George] But if you introduce him to twelve men in a private parlor, he will freeze them all into blocks of ice.

38
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.880
[George] Harrison was aware of the criticism, but refused to flatter or dissemble.

39
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.080
[George] I cannot play the courtier, he told friends.

40
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.320
[George] I must be myself or nothing.

41
00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] Backslash N backslash N in cabinet meetings.

42
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:36.760
[George] Harrison was crisp and demanding, expecting his department secretaries to know every detail of pending legislation without relying on prepared talking points.

43
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:42.360
[George] He dominated his cabinet through sheer intellectual preparation and mastery of the law.

44
00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:52.920
[George] When the 51st Congress convened in December 1889, Republicans held majorities in both the House and the Senate, but their margin in the House was razor thin.

45
00:05:52.920 --> 00:06:07.000
[George] House Democrats employed the disappearing quorum, a procedural trick where Democratic members sat silently in their seats and refused to answer roll calls, preventing a legislative quorum and completely paralyzing government business.

46
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.320
[George] The brilliant, towering Speaker of the House, Thomas Brackett Reed of Maine, known as Tsar Reed, decided to break the paralysis.

47
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:24.040
[George] On January 29, 1890, Reed ordered the clerk to record the names of Democratic members sitting in the chamber who refused to vote.

48
00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:32.760
[George] When Democrats erupted in howling fury, shouting that the Speaker had no right to count them, Reed smiled calmly from the dais and drawled.

49
00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.800
[George] The chair simply states the incontrovertible fact that the gentleman is present in his seat.

50
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:48.520
[George] With Reed's rules breaking the obstruction, Harrison and Congress embarked on one of the most productive, activist legislative sessions in 19th-century American history.

51
00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:58.440
[George] They passed sweeping veterans pension legislation, modernized naval construction, and enacted massive federal appropriations for river and harbor improvements.

52
00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:10.280
[George] When critics gasped that federal spending had surpassed one billion dollars for the first time in peacetime history, dubbing it the billion Dollar Congress, Speaker Reed delivered his immortal retort.

53
00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:25.280
[George] Yes, but this is a billion-dollar country, backslash and backslash and Speaker Reed's bold reforms permanently ended minority obstruction in the House of Representatives, enabling the Republican majority to enact its sweeping national platform.

54
00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:32.880
[George] The new rules transformed the House of Representatives into an effective governing body capable of responding to national priorities.

55
00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:41.440
[George] The crowning legislative achievement of Harrison's presidency was the passage of the landmark Sherman Antitrust Act in July 1890.

56
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:58.960
[George] In the late 19th century, predatory industrial trusts in oil, steel, sugar, tobacco and whiskey were rapidly monopolizing the American economy, driving small competitors out of business, dictating prices to consumers, and buying state legislatures.

57
00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:11.120
[George] Recognizing that unregulated corporate monopoly threatened the foundational liberty of the Republic, Harrison threw his executive support behind legislation drafted by Senator John Sherman of Ohio.

58
00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.680
[George] The Sherman Act was revolutionary.

59
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:23.360
[George] It declared that every contract, combination in the form of trust or otherwise, or conspiracy, in restraint of trade or commerce among the several states.

60
00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.920
[George] Is hereby declared to be illegal.

61
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:36.720
[George] It was the world's first comprehensive antitrust statute, creating federal criminal penalties and granting the federal government the legal authority to dissolve corporate monopolies.

62
00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:52.160
[George] When President Harrison signed the bill into law on July 2, 1890, he established an enduring constitutional cornerstone that Theodore Roosevelt and William Howard Taft would later wield to break the mighty empires of Standard Oil and J.P.

63
00:08:52.160 --> 00:09:05.720
[George] Morgan, backslash and backslash n the Sherman Antitrust Act was passed by near-unanimous votes in both houses of Congress, demonstrating the overwhelming public demand to reign in predatory monopolistic combinations.

64
00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:13.880
[George] Harrison insisted that the federal government possess the constitutional duty to prevent private syndicates from crushing economic liberty.

65
00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:20.360
[George] Benjamin Harrison's administration also transformed the map and the environment of the United States.

66
00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:27.880
[George] Under Harrison, six new Western states were admitted to the Union, more than under any other president in American history.

67
00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:33.880
[George] North Dakota, South Dakota, Montana, Washington, Idaho, and Wyoming.

68
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:42.600
[George] Harrison personally signed the proclamations, ensuring that the Western frontier was permanently woven into the constitutional fabric of the Republic.

69
00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.480
[George] Furthermore, Harrison was an unsung pioneer of American conservation.

70
00:09:47.480 --> 00:10:00.680
[George] Appalled by the rampant clear cutting of ancient Western forests by commercial timber syndicates, Harrison worked with Congress to pass the Land Revision Act of 1891, which included the historic Forest Reserve Act.

71
00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:18.040
[George] Harrison used this authority boldly, issuing executive proclamations that set aside more than 22 million acres of pristine public lands as permanent forest reserves, including the lands that would become Yellowstone National Park, Grand Canyon National Park, and Yosemite.

72
00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:29.240
[George] Long before Theodore Roosevelt popularized environmental conservation, Benjamin Harrison established the foundational framework of the United States National Forest System.

73
00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:38.200
[George] Harrison's visionary creation of the forest reserves protected millions of acres of Western timberland from exploitation.

74
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:41.800
[George] Preserving pristine watersheds for future generations.

75
00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:49.440
[George] His decisive executive orders laid the foundation for the American conservation movement decades before the term became popular.

76
00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.200
[George] Yet Harrison's ambitious legislative agenda carried severe political consequences.

77
00:10:55.200 --> 00:11:03.200
[George] In October 1890, Congress passed the aggressive McKinley Tariff, sponsored by Ohio Congressman William McKinley.

78
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.880
[George] The tariff raised average duties on foreign imports to an astonishing 49%.

79
00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:23.440
[George] While intended to protect domestic wages, the immediate result was a sharp, nationwide spike in the prices of everyday consumer goods, from kitchen utensils to work boots, just weeks before the 1890 midterm elections.

80
00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:30.400
[George] Consumers were outraged, and retail merchants posted signs blaming the Republican tariff for higher prices.

81
00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.360
[George] The voters rendered a devastating verdict.

82
00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:45.840
[George] In November 1890, Democrats swept the midterm elections in a historic landslide, wiping out the Republican House majority and leaving Harrison politically isolated for the second half of his term.

83
00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.840
[George] Yet Benjamin Harrison never expressed regret for his policies.

84
00:11:49.840 --> 00:12:02.320
[George] He had governed with deep intellectual conviction, modernized the Navy, established the legal supremacy of the federal government over corporate monopolies, and expanded the American Union across the West.

85
00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:08.240
[George] He was an honorable, principled statesman who chose bold national progress over political safety.

86
00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:19.840
[George] Backslash N backslash N although the midterm backlash was severe, the legislative framework established by Harrison and the 51st Congress shaped American economic life for decades.

87
00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:27.440
[George] Harrison accepted the political verdict calmly, convinced that history would vindicate his administration's courage.

88
00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:33.800
[Rebecca] Benjamin Harrison's presidency bridged the frontier era and the modern industrial state.

89
00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:46.360
[Rebecca] By signing the Sherman Antitrust Act and pioneering Federal Forest Reserves, he proved that the federal government could act decisively to protect the public interest against predatory monopolies.

90
00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:59.240
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our portrait of Harrison with his trailblazing foreign policy, his visionary Pan American conferences, and the heartbreaking personal tragedy that overshadowed the election of 1892.

91
00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:03.800
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

